## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of per-tenant privacy controls, simple API design, and ingest-time auditing with a concrete example.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy and versioning
- Regex-based redaction and field-level redaction
- Lightweight audit logging without storing raw prompts

## Code Example

```javascript
function sanitizePrompt(tenantPolicy, prompt) {
  let redacted = prompt;
  for (const r of tenantPolicy.redactRegexes) {
    redacted = redacted.replace(new RegExp(r, 'g'), '[REDACTED]');
  }
  tenantPolicy.redactFields.forEach(f => {
    const re = new RegExp(`"${f}"\s*:\s*"[^"]*"`, 'g');
    redacted = redacted.replace(re, `"${f}":"[REDACTED]"`);
  });
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test performance under bursty load?
- How would policy updates drift affect in-flight requests and how would you mitigate?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about lightweight, policy-driven routing in a multi-tenant LLM ops context. Focuses on practical data modeling, decision logic, and test coverage for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant routing state (budget, privacy)
- Latency estimation and budget checks
- Policy-compliant model selection and auditing
- Basic test plan with reproducible scenarios

## Code Example
```javascript
function routeForTenant(tenant, models, estimates, budgets){
  // pick fastest model within budget and privacy constraints
  const allowed = models.filter(m => tenant.allowedModels.includes(m.name) && m.privacyOK === true);
  const feasible = allowed.filter(m => estimates[m.name] <= tenant.latencyBudgetMs);
  return feasible.sort((a,b) => estimates[a.name]-estimates[b.name])[0] || null;
}
```

